```

So the loss is real.

PyTorch backpropagates through:

* real part
* imaginary part
* conjugation
* multiplication

and produces real gradients with respect to your real RBM parameters.

This is perfectly valid real calculus:

You are effectively optimizing:

$$
\mathcal{L}(\theta)
===================

\log \left(
\left|
\sum_k f_k(\theta)
\right|^2
\right)
$$

where each ( f_k(\theta) ) is complex-valued but built from real parameters.

That’s totally legitimate.

